+++
title = "Static Compression"
template = "docs"
+++

Revela can pre-compress your static files with **Gzip** and **Brotli** for faster loading.

> **Do you need this?** If you're using GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, or Cloudflare Pages – **no!** These platforms compress automatically. This plugin is for self-hosted servers, AWS S3, or basic web hosts.

<div class="features-grid">

### 📦 Pre-Generated

Compressed files created at build time, not on every request.

### 🚀 About 80% Smaller

HTML, CSS, and JS files shrink a lot with Brotli: about 80% for this website's own pages (HTML −80%, CSS −78%, JS −69%).

### ⚡ No Work per Request

Servers send the pre-compressed files as they are instead of compressing every request.

</div>

## Installation

The **Standalone** edition has Compress built in. In the **Full** edition, install it from the bundled packages:

```bash
revela plugin install Compress
```

## Usage

### Compress Your Site

```bash
# After generating your site
revela generate compress
```

> **Note:** Compression runs separately, not as part of `generate all`.
> Your server must be configured to serve pre-compressed files.

In a terminal, compression shows a live progress bar; in CI logs or redirected output it prints plain progress lines instead (one about every 10 %).

Running it again only compresses what changed. Revela keeps a `.gz`/`.br` file when the page it was made from has exactly the same content as last time and the compressed file itself is untouched; everything else is compressed again. The summary shows how many files were compressed and how many were unchanged.

> **Note:** `revela generate pages` (and therefore `generate all`) removes all compressed files before it writes new pages, so no outdated `.gz`/`.br` file can be served. After such a build, the next `revela generate compress` compresses everything again.

The local `revela serve` preview server automatically negotiates existing `.br` and `.gz`
variants using the browser's `Accept-Encoding` header. Production servers still need their
equivalent static Brotli/Gzip configuration.

### Remove Compressed Files

```bash
revela clean compress
```

## What Gets Compressed?

| File Type | Typical Savings |
|-----------|-----------------|
| HTML | 70-85% |
| CSS | 75-90% |
| JavaScript | 65-80% |
| JSON | 70-85% |
| SVG | 50-70% |
| XML | 70-85% |

**Images are skipped** – they're already optimized (AVIF, WebP, JPG).

## Output Structure

Your files remain unchanged. Compressed versions are added alongside:

```
output/
├── index.html          ← Original (10 KB)
├── index.html.gz       ← Gzip (2.5 KB)
├── index.html.br       ← Brotli (2.1 KB)
├── _assets/
│   ├── main.css
│   ├── main.css.gz
│   └── main.css.br
```

Revela remembers which `.gz` and `.br` files it created in `.revela/compress/ownership.json` in your project, not in the output, so that record is never uploaded with your site. Together with each file it stores a fingerprint of the page it was compressed from, which is how it recognises unchanged pages. `revela clean compress` removes only those files and never touches other `.gz`/`.br` files you put there yourself.

